      "latency_ms": 2039.7,
      "prompt_tokens": 85,
      "completion_tokens": 841,
      "output": "**Water** is a simple chemical compound that is essential for life on Earth. Its basic characteristics are:\n\n| Aspect | Details |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Chemical formula** | **H₂O** – two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom. |\n| **Molecular structure** | Bent (angular) shape with a bond angle of about 104.5°, giving the molecule a permanent dipole (partial positive charge on the hydrogens, partial negative charge on the oxygen). |\n| **Physical states** | Exists naturally as **solid (ice), liquid (water), and gas (water vapor/steam)**. The transitions between these phases occur at 0 °C (freezing/melting) and 100 °C (boiling) at 1 atm pressure. |\n| **Key properties** | • **High specific heat** – can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature rises, helping regulate climate and body temperature.<br>• **High heat of vaporization** – requires a lot of energy to evaporate, driving cooling processes like sweating.<br>• **Universal solvent** – polar nature dissolves many ionic and polar substances, making it the “solvent of life.”<br>• **Surface tension** – strong cohesion between water molecules creates a “skin” that allows insects to walk on water and plants to transport water upward (capillary action).<br>• **Density anomaly** – water is most dense at 4 °C; ice is less dense than liquid water, so it floats, insulating aquatic life in cold climates. |\n| **Occurrence** | Covers ~71 % of Earth’s surface (≈ 97 % of it is saltwater in oceans). Freshwater (rivers, lakes, groundwater, glaciers) makes up only ~2.5 % of total water, and most of that is locked in ice or deep underground. |\n| **Biological importance** | • **Medium for biochemical reactions** – enzymes, metabolism, DNA replication, etc., all occur in aqueous environments.<br>• **Transport** – blood, sap, and cellular fluids rely on water to move nutrients, waste, and gases.<br>• **Structural role** – water provides turgor pressure in plant cells and contributes to the shape of many organisms. |\n| **Industrial & everyday uses** | Drinking, agriculture, sanitation, power generation (hydropower, steam turbines), manufacturing (solvent, coolant), chemical synthesis, fire fighting, recreation, etc. |\n| **Environmental role** | • **Climate regulator** – oceans store and redistribute heat; water vapor is a major greenhouse gas.<br>• **Weather formation** – evaporation, condensation, and precipitation drive the water cycle.<br>• **Erosion and sediment transport** – rivers and glaciers shape landscapes. |\n\n### Why water is unique\n- **Polarity + hydrogen bonding:** Each water molecule can form up to four hydrogen bonds with neighbors, creating a dynamic, cohesive network that gives water its high surface tension, high boiling point relative to its molecular weight, and its ability to dissolve many substances.\n- **Self‑ionization:** A tiny fraction of water molecules spontaneously dissociate into H⁺ (actually H₃O⁺) and OH⁻, giving pure water a neutral pH of 7. This property underlies acid–base chemistry.\n- **Versatile solvent:** Because of its polarity, water can solvate both positively and negatively charged ions and many polar organic molecules, making it the medium in which most biological and many chemical processes occur.\n\n### In a nutshell\nWater is a tiny, polar molecule (H₂O) that can exist as ice, liquid, or vapor under Earth‑like conditions. Its exceptional thermal, mechanical, and chemical properties arise from hydrogen bonding, making it indispensable for life, climate regulation, and countless human activities."
